"closed_at":"2026-06-19T16:54:35Z"},"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-01-24T00:32:22Z","updated_at":"2026-03-13T17:19:04Z","closed_at":"2026-03-13T17:18:22Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducable in 4.6.rc1\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.6.rc1 - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1060 6GB (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.7628) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-7500 CPU @ 3.40GHz (4 threads) - 15.96 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen calling `FABRIK3D.set_target_node`, there's no warning or error when set to a `NodePath` that does not exist. I'm not sure what the desired behaviour should be (it's my first issue), but I assume there should be some sort of user feedback, so somebody doesn't spend an hour on a very hot day trying to figure out why only some targets are working.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nI have attached an MRP that has an arm that points at two targets, then attempts to point to a third with an invalid name.\n\nOtherwise, to replicate from scratch:\n1. Create a Skeleton3D\n2. Add a FABRIK3D node and add a setting for your bone chain.\n3. Add a target to that setting and see how the IK points to the target.\n4. In code, call `FABRIK3D.set_target_node(0, ^\"invalid_node_path\")` and see how the `Target Node` setting is set to the invalid `NodePath`, but there is no change to the bone positions, and no error or warning shown to the user.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[mrp-fabrik-ik-node-path-validity.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/24832491/mrp-fabrik-ik-node-path-validity.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubuserco